 "reading": [
  "The proof that checks here is a modified copy of OpenAI's and Hawkley's: 20 of its 3,210 files were changed to build on the newer Lean and Mathlib (every change listed with both sha256s in OpenAIEth/PORT.md, and every file checked here against the scout pins: byte-equal to its pin, or modified exactly as declared, nothing else). Lean checks whatever proof is there against the unchanged statements (Hawkley's two theorem types printed by our file, MainResults.lean byte-equal to Anthropic's), so the port changes nothing that is decided; it is said here because the proof is not OpenAI's or Hawkley's bytes.",
  "This closes the version seam on OpenAI's side the way Anthropic's was closed (certs/eth-chain.json): by rebuilding the proof in the statement's Lean (v4.36.0-rc2), not by comparing kernels across v4.34.1 → v4.36.0-rc2. That complexitylib's ETH is the textbook ETH stays a reading of a definition.",
  "Lean's judge ran without its sandbox (bubblewrap cannot create a user namespace on RunPod's containers). The sandbox protects the judge from a hostile build; the build here is our own claimbox's.",
  "The judge's --paranoid run did not finish on this export (2.45 GB; the Anthropic chain's was 0.65 GB and all six checkers accepted there): the bundled nanoda 0.4.17 overflows a worker thread's stack and has no stack setting, lean4lean's fuel counter reaches zero, con-leche and con-ron exhaust a compiled fuel constant — each stop reproduced by hand on the comparator's own export (judge.diagnosis). The verdict reads the judge re-run with checkers that can finish: leanchecker-paranoid, our pinned nanoda with a 1 GiB thread stack, lean4lean with its fuel raised 100×, and Lean's default kernel. con-leche and con-ron are not among them; nothing here is their verdict either way. The amendment is dated in the pre-registration.",
  "claimbox and cert-machine have the same operator: this decision separates the roles of author and checker; it is not a second party.",
  "After the record, on the con-leche maintainer's request (leanprover/con-leche#16: \"Could you try latest master?\"), con-leche and con-ron built from source at their master branches (con-leche 7cb7a45c (2026-11-05 16:20 UTC) and dc8ca857 (2026-11-05 22:40 UTC); con-ron 97eead2d (2026-10-31 16:17 UTC)) and run by hand on the same export (the comparator's bytes, sha256 checked) accept all 98,686 declarations in every run that had the memory: con-leche at 4–32 workers in both modes, 130–287 s, 34.3–68.9 GiB; con-ron 63–136 s, 41.0–41.4 GiB. At 16 workers con-leche was killed by a 64 GB machine's memory limit in both modes (signal 9, nothing printed; a resource fact, not a verdict); with fewer workers it fits: 8 workers 51.4 GiB, 4 workers 34.3 GiB. On the second export (OpenAI's Main with its challenge theorem exact_count_and_sat, sha256 a8afc439…), where the bundled builds stopped at QueryNodeEvaluation.nodeBody_prefix, con-leche (both modes) and con-ron accept all 93,373 declarations (42.0–73.2 GiB). Both exports are public as a regression test for the checkers: doi:10.5281/zenodo.23991348. The fuel stops recorded above are fixed on their master branches, not in the builds Lean v4.36.0-rc2 bundles. Outside the judge: not part of the decision."
